From 6c213940e2bbb2266ff9986884c73d4809142f34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 20:01:03 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] rocket RK3576: derive the first conv's requant, with four
 more bits of shift

The literals were offset -2, scale 0x5391 and shift 25, and the comment above
them said computing them instead gives offset -128, scale 0x76be and shift 22,
which is wrong for conv0. Two thirds of that is backwards.

With an impulse first conv, whose output decodes to exactly the right input
plane and tap on all 32 channels, the map from the hardware's surface to the
reference is affine and identical for every channel. Fitted over unclipped
pixels only:

    shift 25   a 0.7096   b  -89.34    b/a = -125.90
    shift 26   a 1.4197   b -178.84    b/a = -125.97
    shift 27   a 2.8369   b -357.38    b/a = -125.98
    shift 28   a 5.6522   b -711.90    b/a = -125.95

correlation 0.9999, and a doubles with every halving of the multiplier, so it
is the ratio between what is written and what is wanted and it survived being
halved three times.

The multiplier wanted is 0.000452413 while in_sc * wt_sc / out_sc is
0.007247427, a ratio of 16.02: the generic computation with four more bits of
shift, presumably because this path enables the CNA CVT, 0x104c = 0x4000
against the generic bypass, leaving the accumulator four bits hot. And b/a is a
constant -126, exactly the gap between the offset written, -2, and the generic
out_zp - 0x80 of -128.

So the offset and the scale in that comment were right and the shift was short
by four; reading the three together as one captured triple is what kept conv0
broken. conv0 now gets scale 0x76be at shift 26 with offset -128, 0.12 percent
from the measured multiplier.

ROCKET_FC_OFFSET, ROCKET_FC_SCALE and ROCKET_FC_SHIFT still override so the old
triple can be put back as a control.

Signed-off-by: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c | 62 ++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 51 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c
index 317c609..acd5469 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c
@@ -260,18 +260,58 @@ fill_first_regcmd_rk3576_firstconv(struct rkt_ml_subgraph *subgraph,
    R_DPU(0x40a4, 0x80000000);
    R_DPU(0x40a8, 0x7fffffff);
    /*
-    * OUT_CVT requant for conv0, vendor-exact. A register-by-register diff of
-    * the live vendor RK3576 conv0 regcmd against this encoder showed these three
-    * are this conv0's real captured values (offset -2, scale 0x5391, shift 25) --
-    * NOT, as a previous comment guessed, leftovers from a random-weight conv.
-    * Computing them instead (the block that used to live here) put offset -128 /
-    * scale 0x76be / shift 22, which is wrong for conv0. The output saturation we
-    * chased to here was actually the CNA FC_CON1=0x777 feature-offset bug below;
-    * with that fixed these vendor values are the correct requant.
+    * OUT_CVT requant for the first conv: the generic computation with the
+    * shift four larger.
+    *
+    * The literals here were offset -2, scale 0x5391 and shift 25, and the
+    * comment that stood above them said computing them instead gives offset
+    * -128, scale 0x76be and shift 22, "which is wrong for conv0". Two thirds
+    * of that is now measured to be backwards.
+    *
+    * With an impulse first conv, whose output decodes to exactly the right
+    * input plane and tap on all 32 channels, the map from the hardware's
+    * surface to the reference is affine and the same for every channel. Fitted
+    * over unclipped pixels only:
+    *
+    *   shift 25   a 0.7096   b  -89.34     b/a = -125.90
+    *   shift 26   a 1.4197   b -178.84     b/a = -125.97
+    *   shift 27   a 2.8369   b -357.38     b/a = -125.98
+    *   shift 28   a 5.6522   b -711.90     b/a = -125.95
+    *
+    * correlation 0.9999, and a doubles with every halving of the multiplier,
+    * so a IS the ratio between the multiplier written and the one wanted, and
+    * it survived being halved three times.
+    *
+    * Two things fall out of it. The multiplier wanted is 0.000452413, and
+    * in_sc * wt_sc / out_sc is 0.007247427, a ratio of 16.02: the generic
+    * value with FOUR more bits of shift. And b/a is a constant -126 at every
+    * shift, which is the hardware adding 126, which is exactly the gap between
+    * the offset written here, -2, and the generic out_zp - 0x80, -128.
+    *
+    * So the old comment had the offset and the scale right and the shift short
+    * by four, and reading the three together as "the captured values" is what
+    * kept conv0 broken. The 16 is presumably the CNA CVT this path enables,
+    * 0x104c = 0x4000 against the generic path's bypass, leaving the
+    * accumulator four bits hot.
+    *
+    * ROCKET_FC_OFFSET, ROCKET_FC_SCALE and ROCKET_FC_SHIFT still override, so
+    * the old triple can be put back as a control.
     */
-   R_DPU(0x40ac, rkt_env_u32("ROCKET_FC_OFFSET", 0xfffffffe)); /* OUT_CVT offset */
-   R_DPU(0x40b0, rkt_env_u32("ROCKET_FC_SCALE", 0x00005391));  /* OUT_CVT requant scale */
-   R_DPU(0x40b4, rkt_env_u32("ROCKET_FC_SHIFT", 0x00000019));  /* OUT_CVT shift */
+   {
+      float conv_scale =
+         (task->input_scale * task->weights_scale) / task->output_scale;
+      uint32_t sb = fui(conv_scale);
+      /* The generic path computes this then emits shift - 1; keep both. */
+      unsigned fc_shift = 127 + 31 - 32 - (sb >> 23) + 16 - 1 + 4;
+      unsigned fc_scale = ((sb >> 9) & 0x7fff) + 1;
+      if (fc_scale < (1 << 14))
+         fc_scale |= (1 << 14);
+      int32_t fc_off = (int32_t)task->output_zero_point - 0x80;
+
+      R_DPU(0x40ac, rkt_env_u32("ROCKET_FC_OFFSET", (uint32_t)fc_off));
+      R_DPU(0x40b0, rkt_env_u32("ROCKET_FC_SCALE", fc_scale));
+      R_DPU(0x40b4, rkt_env_u32("ROCKET_FC_SHIFT", fc_shift));
+   }
    R_DPU(0x40b8, 0x00003100); /* out_w*out_h */
    R_DPU(0x40bc, 0x00000000);
    R_DPU(0x40c0, 0x04440100);
-- 
2.43.0

